# Local tooling / clone artifacts -- these are never part of a paper and
# should never be committed. A stray `.claude/worktrees/<id>` gitlink
# (mode 160000, a dangling submodule reference with no .gitmodules entry)
# was committed by accident in the very first commit of this repo and has
# been removed from tracking -- see the 2026-08 constellation-automation
# cleanup pass. This line exists so it, or anything like it, can't recur.
.claude/

# Steering vectors (REDACTED - do not commit)
*.pt
calibration.pt

# Audit evidence — files force-committed past ignore rules must not be deleted
# Add exemptions here as papers graduate with audit logs:
# !paper-name/path/to/evidence.log

# Policy, not a pattern: never commit a renamed/"pretty" duplicate copy of a
# paper's PDF (e.g. a root-level Some_Paper_-_Academic_Version.pdf) for the
# sake of a nicer download-link filename on the lab site. That is exactly
# how the 2026-08 stale-link bug happened -- the duplicate stopped getting
# rebuilt while the real per-paper file underneath it kept improving, and
# the site kept serving the stale copy. Site cards link straight to the
# canonical file's real path in this repo (or its real external home, e.g.
# Zenodo) -- no copy, no extra click. See
# research_constellation_automation_README.md.

# Correction-sweep backups. By 2026-09-09 these had reached 134 files / 8.4 MB
# -- 83% of everything untracked in the repo -- of which 18 were *.pdf.bak-*.
# PDFs are binary and undeltifiable, so committing those alone would have added
# ~5.1 MB to every clone, permanently. Git history is the backup; a .bak beside
# the file is a working-tree convenience with no business in the index.
#
# Policy, not a pattern: if a .bak is ever cited as EVIDENCE by an audit
# document (the 2026-09-03 byline sweep diffed against *.bak-byline-20260903,
# for example), force-add that specific file and add a "!" exemption here with
# a note. Evidence a report depends on must not be silently ignorable.
*.bak
*.bak*
